  - 5,200,000 / 45,000 ≈ 115.6 months (≈ 9.6 years)

Which is correct: (A) the 16-month runway figure, or (B) the $45K/month burn? Or do these refer to different measures (e.g., $45K = discretionary/monthly opex reduction target, or $45K = “cash-only” burn excluding non-cash items, or $45K = incremental burn after recent changes)? Also confirm whether the $5.2M cash is unrestricted operating cash (or if part is restricted or earmarked for capex, taxes, AR reserves, etc.).

Below I give both a clean, board-ready narrative assuming the 16-month runway is the true metric, plus a second short note you can use if the $45K figure is the one you meant. I also include suggested supporting analysis and recommended next steps.

Executive summary — (assuming 16 months runway is correct)
- Current position: Horizon Media has $5.2M in cash, giving approximately 16 months of runway at the current net cash burn.
- Implied burn: That implies a net cash burn of ~$325K/month. Against last quarter revenue of $2.4M (≈ $800K/month), net burn is ~40% of monthly revenue.
- Drivers: The net cash burn reflects operating expenses (personnel, media buying gaps, fixed overhead), timing of receivables and payables, and any one-time items charged this period. Revenue last quarter ($2.4M) provides solid top-line, but cadence/seasonality or delayed collections are increasing short-term funding requirements.
- Trend: If current revenue and expense patterns hold, cash will decline steadily and require financing or cost reduction before runway compresses below a 12-month cushion (common board threshold). A 16-month runway is acceptable but leaves limited room for downside shocks (client losses, slower collections, campaign timing shifts).
- Ask / action: We should (1) reconcile and validate the $325K monthly net burn breakdown (fixed vs variable, recurring vs one-time), (2) run a 12-month cash-flow forecast with AR/AP timing and worst-case revenue stress, and (3) prepare contingency actions (cost reductions, credit line / bridge options, improved collections).

Concise narrative paragraph for internal materials (board / exec summary)
- “As of [date], Horizon Media holds $5.2M in cash, which provides ~16 months of runway at our current net cash burn. The implied monthly net cash burn is ~$325K, driven by operating costs and the timing of client receipts. Last quarter revenue was $2.4M (~$800K/month), so our current net cash burn equals roughly 40% of monthly revenue. We recommend reconciling the burn drivers, completing a 12-month cash-flow forecast under base/downside/upside scenarios, and preparing contingency measures (collections acceleration, discretionary cost cuts, and short-term financing options) to preserve optionality and extend runway.”

Alternative short note — (if $45K/month burn is the true net burn)
- “If our true net burn is $45K/month, the $5.2M cash balance implies ~116 months of runway, which would be an exceptionally strong liquidity position. We should confirm that $45K refers to fully loaded net cash burn (after all revenues and recurring expenses) and verify whether any one-time liabilities or working capital needs are excluded.”

Suggested detailed support & analysis to include in internal packet
- A 12-month monthly cash-flow table (opening cash, incoming receipts by client & timing, gross cash inflows from sales, cash outflows by line: payroll, rent, media buys, vendor payments, capex, taxes).
- Reconciliation of the “burn” number: show how you get from operating P&L to net cash burn (add back non-cash items like depreciation, adjust for changes in working capital and one-off items).
- Sensitivity scenarios (example):
  - Base: current run-rate (net burn = $325K) → runway 16 months
  - Upside: reduce discretionary spend 20% → net burn ≈ $260K → runway ≈ 20 months
  - Downside: revenue drops 30% (collections lag) → net burn ≈ $565K → runway ≈ 9 months
- Key KPIs: cash days on hand, cash burn as % of monthly revenue, revenue concentration (top 5 clients), DSO and DPO trends.

Recommended actions (immediate and near-term)
- Immediate (next 30 days):
