- One-paragraph summary:
  “As of [date], Horizon Media holds $5.2M of cash, providing ~16 months of runway at the current burn profile. This implies an effective net cash burn of ~ $325K/month. Last quarter revenue was $2.4M (monthly run rate ~$800K). While revenue levels are healthy, the cash outflow profile (or planned investments) consumes a material portion of cash and requires continued monitoring of cost drivers and collections to preserve runway.”
- Key drivers/notes:
  - Implied net burn: $325K/month — detail whether this includes planned investments (e.g., product launches, hiring, marketing) or is recurring.
  - Revenue vs burn: monthly revenue (~$800K) still exceeds the implied net burn, which suggests either revenue is not being collected timely or burn definition is gross cash outflows (pre-revenue offset). Clarify AR days, deferred revenue, and one-off items.
  - Risks: upcoming contract renewals, seasonal revenue declines, large one-time payments (rent, bonuses).
- Recommended actions:
  - Break out gross cash outflows and cash inflows in the model to show the source of the $325K net burn.
  - Build 3-case cash forecast (0–12 months weekly/monthly granularity) highlighting the timing sensitivity of receivables and major payables.
  - If the 16-month runway includes planned hires/investments, prepare a plan to stretch runway (defer non-critical spend) or a fundraising contingency (term sheet targets and timing).

