Scenario A — Using the 16-month runway (implied net burn ≈ $325K/month)
Calculations
- Monthly revenue (last quarter average): $2.4M / 3 = $800K
- Implied monthly net burn = 5.2M / 16 = $325K
- Implied monthly operating spend = revenue + burn = $800K + $325K = $1,125K

Quick sensitivity (examples)
- Upside: +20% revenue → revenue = $960K → net burn = 1,125K − 960K = $165K → runway ≈ 31.5 months
- Downside: −25% revenue → revenue = $600K → net burn = 1,125K − 600K = $525K → runway ≈ 9.9 months
- Cost reduction: −15% operating spend → expenses = 1,125K × 0.85 = 956.25K → net burn = 156.25K → runway ≈ 33.3 months

Narrative (internal / investor-ready) — conservative (uses 16-month runway)
“Horizon Media currently holds $5.2M in cash and generated $2.4M in revenue last quarter (≈$800K/month). Based on our current expense profile, we are running an implied net cash burn of approximately $325K/month, which yields roughly 16 months of runway. This runway gives us time to execute on growth initiatives, but it also means we must prioritize near-term milestones that materially improve top-line performance or materially reduce operating spend. Under a moderate upside (20% revenue growth) our runway extends to ~31 months; under a 25% revenue decline it shortens to ~10 months. Recommended focus areas are (1) accelerate revenue conversion on existing channels to protect monthly revenue, (2) defer or stage noncritical spend so we can conserve cash if needed, and (3) prepare a raise or bridge plan timed intentionally to reach an improved valuation/traction point.”

Scenario B — If burn really is $45K/month (the alternative)
Calculations
- Runway with $45K/month = 5.2M / 45K ≈ 115.6 months (~9.6 years)
- With revenue of $800K/month and net burn of only $45K/month, operating spend ≈ $845K/month.

Narrative — optimistic (uses $45K burn)
“Horizon Media has $5.2M cash and generated $2.4M last quarter. With an estimated net cash burn of only $45K/month, we have substantial runway (~9.5 years). This gives us strong flexibility to invest in growth. That said, we should still monitor revenue trends closely and document the assumptions behind the low burn (are any costs off-balance-sheet or one-time?). We recommend running a baseline plus downside scenarios to verify runway under revenue stress and to schedule fundraising only as needed to capture growth opportunities.”

Key messages / talking points to include
- State the cash balance, trailing revenue (quarterly and monthly), and the source/definition of “burn” (net cash flow vs. operating expense).
- Show implied runway and how it was calculated (cash ÷ net burn).
- Present 3 scenarios (base, downside, upside) with months of runway and key triggers for action (e.g., <12 months runway → start fundraising).
- Call out one-time items or nonrecurring receipts/expenses that distort burn.
- Recommended next steps: prepare 12–24 month cash-flow forecast, sensitivity table, and a “fundraising trigger” timeline.

Suggested slides/attachments to support the narrative
- Monthly cash-balance projection for 24 months (base/downside/upside)
- Revenue vs. expenses (monthly) and net burn chart (last 6–12 months)
