- Base case: burn = $45K → runway ≈ 115 months.
- Stress case: if net burn rises to $200K/month → runway = 5.2M ÷ 200K = 26 months.
- Revenue shock: if revenue declines 30% (from $800K/month to $560K/month) and burn rises modestly (+$50K) update sensitivity table accordingly.
Recommended asks
- Confirm AR aging and cash collection schedule.
- Approve up to $X for strategic investments only after ROI review.

B — If runway = 16 months (implied net burn ≈ $325K/month)
Executive summary (short)
- Horizon Media has $5.2M in cash and an effective runway of 16 months based on current cash flow dynamics, implying a net cash burn around $325K/month. Revenue last quarter was $2.4M, but current cash outflows (including operating outflows, one-off items, or financing costs) are driving a materially negative net cash position over time.
Key takeaways and interpretation
- Runway of 16 months requires active runway management and near-term planning for either reducing burn or raising capital. We should target extending runway to at least 24 months to provide more strategic flexibility.
- Investigate drivers of the high net burn given strong reported revenue last quarter -- likely causes include: delayed cash collections/AR aging, large capex or working capital build, one-time payouts, or debt service.
Suggested metrics and sensitivities to include
- Base case: net burn ≈ $325K/month → 16 months runway (5.2M ÷ 325K).
- Mitigation example 1: reduce monthly burn to $200K → runway ≈ 26 months.
